//? Title: Hypothesis Tests: Null Models, Effects and Calibration
//? Subtitle: Worked t, rank, ANOVA, binomial and Fisher calculations before an iris comparison
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Observed differences can arise from sampling variation, systematic effects or a poor
sampling model. Given data and a specified null model, a hypothesis test measures how
extreme a chosen statistic would be under that model; it does not assign a probability
to the null being true. We derive and check classical mean, rank, correlation, variance,
shape and count tests using small examples before examining iris measurements.
Exact enumeration exposes the conservatism of discrete tests and the limitations of a
normal approximation. A binomial counterexample shows why an exact two-sided p-value and
an equal-tailed confidence interval need not give matching decisions. The iris analysis
reports effect sizes, intervals and a declared four-test Holm family, distinguishes
pooled from within-species association, and avoids treating a fitted-normal KS p-value as
valid evidence. Small representable p-values are retained and printed in scientific notation.
)

\toc

> The method

>> Specify the sampling model and question first

For a statistic \(T\) and a prespecified ordering of extreme outcomes, a p-value is a
null probability such as \(p=P_0\{T\ge T_{\rm obs}\}\). Under a valid null model it
satisfies \(P_0(p\le\alpha)\le\alpha\). Equality is typical for continuous exact
references; discreteness can make the inequality strict. This calibration is a repeated-
sampling property, not \(P(H_0\mid\text{data})\), the probability of replication, or
the size of the effect. Failure to reject does not prove equality or equivalence.

Choose the outcome, groups, direction, sampling unit and multiplicity family before
inspecting p-values. Independent flowers, paired repeated measurements and conditional
count tables are different designs. A paired test uses within-pair differences, and
pairing cannot be manufactured by sorting two independent samples. Selection after seeing
outcomes requires additional inference; changing tests until one rejects invalidates a
single-test error interpretation.

The common `HypothesisTestResult` owns a method, statistic, parameters, p-value, estimates
and optional confidence bounds. Field meanings are test-specific: a Wilcoxon statistic
is a rank sum/U, not a z score; a Spearman statistic here is its t approximation, not rho.
`hypothesis_test_table()` gathers results, leaving absent intervals null. Vector APIs
reject nonfinite values rather than silently dropping them. Any complete-case selection
must preserve paired-row alignment and be described by the caller.

>> Mean and variance references

For a one-sample mean, normal independent observations give

$$
 \bar x=\frac1n\sum_i x_i,\quad s^2=\frac{\sum_i(x_i-\bar x)^2}{n-1},\quad
 T=\frac{\bar x-\mu_0}{s/\sqrt n}\sim t_{n-1},\quad
 C_{1-\alpha}=\bar x\pm t_{1-\alpha/2,n-1}\frac{s}{\sqrt n}.
$$
\alttext(The one-sample t statistic divides the mean minus its null value by the estimated standard error. Its two-sided confidence interval uses a Student critical value with n minus one degrees of freedom.)

The normal mean and residual sum of squares are independent; their standardized normal
and chi-square ratio gives the Student reference. A paired t-test applies the same result
to \(d_i=x_i-y_i\). The differences need the distributional assumption, not each marginal
measurement separately. Under the same normal model, the two-sided mean interval excludes
\(\mu_0\) exactly when its matching two-sided t-test rejects.

For independent groups, Welch estimates the variance of the mean difference and its df:

$$
 v_x=s_x^2/n_x,\quad v_y=s_y^2/n_y,\qquad
 T_W=\frac{\bar x-\bar y}{\sqrt{v_x+v_y}},\quad
 \nu=\frac{(v_x+v_y)^2}{v_x^2/(n_x-1)+v_y^2/(n_y-1)}.
$$
\alttext(Welch uses the sum of the groups' estimated mean variances as squared standard error, with Satterthwaite degrees of freedom obtained by moment matching.)

This is an approximate t reference even for unequal-variance normal populations. Pooled
Student instead estimates a common variance and uses \(n_x+n_y-2\) df; it requires that
common-variance model. An F-test is not a required gatekeeper before choosing Welch.
For variance comparison itself, independent normal samples give
\(s_x^2/s_y^2\sim F_{n_x-1,n_y-1}\); its confidence interval targets the variance ratio.
That reference is especially sensitive to nonnormality.

Classical one-way ANOVA uses a common within-group variance:

$$
 SS_B=\sum_g n_g(\bar x_g-\bar x)^2,\quad
 SS_W=\sum_{g,i}(x_{gi}-\bar x_g)^2,\quad
 F=\frac{SS_B/(k-1)}{SS_W/(N-k)}\sim F_{k-1,N-k}.
$$
\alttext(ANOVA compares the between-group mean square with the within-group mean square. Normal independent groups with a common variance give an F reference.)

The orthogonal between/within projections yield independent scaled chi-squares under
that null. `one_way_anova()` is this equal-variance procedure, not Welch ANOVA.
The returned F-test does not identify which group differs or estimate a causal effect.

>> Ranks answer a different question

Pool two samples and give tied observations their average one-based ranks. The rank-sum
statistic for the first sample is

$$
 U=\sum_{i\in x}R_i-\frac{n_x(n_x+1)}2,\quad E_0(U)=\frac{n_xn_y}2,\quad
 V_0(U)=\frac{n_xn_y}{12}\left[N+1-\frac{\sum_j(t_j^3-t_j)}{N(N-1)}\right].
$$
\alttext(The Mann-Whitney U statistic subtracts the first sample's minimum rank sum. Its null variance uses pooled sample size and a correction for tie groups of sizes t j.)

The implementation subtracts a half-unit continuity correction towards the null centre
for a two-sided z score and uses the normal reference at every sample size. It does not
switch to exact enumeration when samples are small. \(U/(n_xn_y)\) estimates
\(P(X>Y)+\tfrac12P(X=Y)\). The usual null is identical independent distributions;
a common-shape location-shift model permits a location interpretation. Equal medians
alone is not sufficient, and a rank test is not a mean test under weaker assumptions.

The signed-rank test ranks nonzero absolute differences and sums ranks with positive
signs. Its null requires a symmetric difference distribution about zero; it is not a
distribution-free test of an arbitrary median. Kruskal–Wallis instead uses group rank sums
\(R_g\), with tie factor \(C=1-\sum_j(t_j^3-t_j)/(N^3-N)\):

$$
 H=\frac{12\sum_g R_g^2/n_g}{N(N+1)}-3(N+1),\qquad
 H_{\rm corrected}=H/C\ \dot\sim\ \chi^2_{k-1}.
$$
\alttext(Kruskal-Wallis compares squared group rank sums, corrects for pooled ties and uses a chi-square approximation with one fewer degree of freedom than groups.)

The identical-distribution null supplies exchangeability. The statistic is sensitive to
rank-location differences; it is not an omnibus detector of every possible distributional
change. Tiny groups can give a poor chi-square approximation.

>> Association and shape

Pearson measures linear association and Spearman measures correlation of average ranks.
For independent bivariate-normal pairs under zero Pearson correlation,
\(T=r\sqrt{(n-2)/(1-r^2)}\sim t_{n-2}\). Pearson's Fisher-z confidence interval is
approximate. Spearman here reuses the t reference for rho; it is not an exact permutation
test. Neither zero Pearson nor zero Spearman correlation generally implies independence.
Mixing groups can produce a large pooled association even when each group's association
is substantially smaller.

Shapiro–Francia uses the squared correlation of ordered data with Blom normal scores,
\(W'=\operatorname{corr}(x_{(i)},\Phi^{-1}((i-3/8)/(n+1/4)))^2\), with Royston's
approximate p-value for \(5\le n\le5000\). It tests a composite normal family.
A large value is not proof of normality; rounding and limited sample size matter.

KS uses \(D=\sup_x|F_n(x)-F_0(x)|\). For a one-sample test its continuous reference
CDF must be fully specified independently of the sample. Fitting the reference mean and
SD from that same sample changes the null distribution: the ordinary KS p-value then
has no advertised calibration. This API uses an asymptotic reference with a finite-n
correction, not exact finite-sample KS or a Lilliefors correction. Ties from measurement
rounding also affect its continuous-null calibration. In iris we show a benchmark D
without interpreting its nominal p-value.

>> Counts, exact probabilities and confidence intervals

For independent Bernoulli trials, \(X\sim\operatorname{Binomial}(n,p_0)\) under the null.
One-sided exact p-values include the observed count. Two-sided `binomial_test()` sums
PMFs no larger than the observed PMF, with relative ordering tolerance \(10^{-7}\):

$$
 p_{\rm exact}(x)=\sum_{k:\,f_0(k)\le f_0(x)} f_0(k),\qquad
 f_0(k)=\binom nk p_0^k(1-p_0)^{n-k}.
$$
\alttext(The two-sided binomial p-value adds probabilities of counts whose null probability is at most the observed count's probability; this need not be twice a single tail.)

Its Clopper–Pearson interval inverts two equal-tailed one-sided binomial bounds. Coverage
is at least nominal under the Bernoulli model, but it does not in general invert this
probability-ordered two-sided test. The score proportion test instead uses the null SE,
optionally a capped half-count correction, and a normal approximation. Its returned Wilson
interval is uncorrected even when the test is corrected, another reason to check the
stated interval/test definitions rather than assuming duality.

For a 2x2 table \((a,b;c,d)\), Fisher conditions on fixed row and column margins. With
\(N=a+b+c+d\), \(r=a+b\) and \(c_1=a+c\), its top-left count has probability
\(P(A=k)=\binom{c_1}k\binom{N-c_1}{r-k}/\binom Nr\). Two-sided ordering again uses
conditional probabilities, not twice an arbitrary tail. The reported sample odds ratio
is \(ad/(bc)\), not R's conditional maximum-likelihood estimate. A zero observed cell
is not automatically a structural impossibility in the population.

Pearson categorical independence uses \(X^2=\sum(O-E)^2/E\), with
\(E_{ij}=O_{i+}O_{+j}/N\) and a chi-square reference. The approximation needs sufficiently
populated expected cells. Yates correction changes the 2x2 statistic; Fisher changes the
reference experiment. Choose these methods for their model and calibration, not whichever
produces the preferred p-value.

>> Multiple comparisons, numerics and computation

For a declared family of \(m\) p-values sorted as \(p_{(1)}\le\cdots\le p_{(m)}\),
Holm reports \(p^{\rm adj}_{(i)}=\min\{1,\max_{j\le i}(m-j+1)p_{(j)}\}\).
The first true-null rejection would require a true-null p-value at most \(\alpha/m_0\),
so a union bound over the \(m_0\) true nulls gives strong family-wise control without
independence. Four separate 95% intervals remain marginal intervals after Holm adjustment;
they do not become simultaneous 95% intervals merely by sharing a table.

Mean/count sufficient-statistic tests cost linear time in observations; rank/shape/KS
sorting costs \(O(n\log n)\). Exact binomial and 2x2 Fisher p-values enumerate their
finite support, costing \(O(n)\) or the number of feasible tables. The complete binomial
rejection table costs \(O(n^2)\); its size/power calculation is deterministic, not Monte Carlo.
Normal tails use erfc, Student tails use symmetry, F tails use a complementary beta
identity and chi-square tails use the upper incomplete gamma. This retains small
representable p-values that would be lost by subtracting a rounded-to-one CDF.

> Simple examples

>> Four observations and a Student tail

For \(x=(1,2,3,4)\) against \(\mu_0=0\), \(\bar x=5/2\),
\(s^2=5/3\), \(SE=\sqrt{5/12}\), \(t=\sqrt{15}\) and df is three.
The two-sided p-value is about 0.03047 and the matching 95% mean interval excludes zero.
This small example relies strongly on the independent normal-sampling model.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hypothesis_t.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
const std::vector<double> x={1,2,3,4};const auto t=dm::stats::t_test_one_sample(x);
  auto data=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();data.points(x,{1,1,1,1},"observations",{37,99,235});
  data.segments({t.conf_int_lower},{.55},{t.conf_int_upper},{.55},{220,38,38},3).points({t.estimate1},{.55},"mean and 95% CI",{220,38,38});
  data.title("Four values and their mean interval").x_label("measurement").y_label("display row").x_limits(-.5,5).y_limits(0,1.6).abline_v(0);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(data).add(dm::stats::t_test_reference_plot(t)).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/hypothesis_t.svg");
  std::print("{}\n",dm::stats::hypothesis_test_table({t},{"mean = 0"}).select({"label","statistic","parameter1","estimate1","ci_lower","ci_upper","p_value"}).to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| label | statistic | parameter1 | estimate1 | ci_lower | ci_upper | p_value |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| mean = 0 | 3.872983 | 3 | 2.5 | 0.445740 | 4.554260 | 0.030466 |

\image(figures/hypothesis_t.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Four raw values, the mean interval and shaded two-sided Student tails. The shaded density is truncated to the displayed range; the table gives the numerical p-value.)
\alttext(A left panel shows values one to four and the mean confidence interval above zero. A right panel shades both tails beyond plus or minus square root fifteen under a three-degree-of-freedom Student density.)

>> Tied ranks can be inspected

For \(x=(1,2,2)\) and \(y=(3,4,5)\), pooled ranks are
\((1,2.5,2.5,4,5,6)\). Thus \(U=6-6=0\), \(E_0(U)=9/2\),
\(V_0(U)=5.1\) and the corrected score is \(-4/\sqrt{5.1}\).
All nine cross-pairs have X below Y, yet three observations per group give limited
resolution. The displayed p-value is a normal approximation, not a tiny-sample exact result.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hypothesis_ranks.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
const std::vector<double> x={1,2,2},y={3,4,5};const auto ranks=dm::stats::rank_sum_table(x,y);
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  for(const auto& group:{std::string("x"),std::string("y")}) {
    const auto d=ranks.filter([&](const auto& r){return r.get_string("group")==group;});
    plot.points(d.numeric_column("value").values(),d.numeric_column("rank").values(),group,group=="x"?dm::RGB{37,99,235}:dm::RGB{220,38,38},6);
  }
  plot.title("Pooled ranks with an average tie").x_label("observed value").y_label("pooled rank").x_limits(.5,5.5).y_limits(0,7.5);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");plot.size(820,470).save_svg("build/hypothesis_ranks.svg");
  std::print("{}\n\n{}\n",ranks.to_md(),dm::stats::hypothesis_test_table({dm::stats::wilcoxon_rank_sum_test(x,y)},{"normal approximation"}).select({"label","statistic","p_value"}).to_md(6));
}
```
