\caption(Fisher sums the two endpoint probabilities to 0.1. The table reports the two approximate chi-square alternatives separately, with their actual statistics.)
\alttext(Four bars have heights 0.05, 0.45, 0.45 and 0.05. The endpoint bars are highlighted as the selected two-sided conditional tail.)

> Checking calibration and power

Exact enumeration provides an independent repeated-sampling question: how often does
this fixed rule reject? For rejection set \(\mathcal R\), compute

$$
 \operatorname{size}=\sum_{x\in\mathcal R}f(x;n,p_0),\qquad
 \operatorname{power}(p_1)=\sum_{x\in\mathcal R}f(x;n,p_1).
$$
\alttext(Test size sums rejection probabilities under the null success probability. Power sums probabilities over the same rejection region under a stated alternative.)

We fix \(p_0=0.1\), \(p_1=0.3\), \(\alpha=0.05\) and a two-sided alternative,
and enumerate every count for each trial number. Exact, uncorrected score and corrected
score rules are all declared in advance. The red uncorrected-score curve shows why a
normal approximation is unreliable with sparse expected successes. The exact size stays
at or below 5%, but power and size have discrete jumps; a continuous smooth-power
intuition can be misleading. These calculations have no Monte Carlo uncertainty.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hypothesis_calibration.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
std::vector<std::size_t> sizes;for(std::size_t n=1;n<=100;++n)sizes.push_back(n);
  const auto plain=dm::stats::binomial_operating_characteristics(sizes,.1,.3,.05,dm::stats::Alternative::TwoSided,false);
  const auto corrected=dm::stats::binomial_operating_characteristics(sizes,.1,.3);const auto n=plain.numeric_column("n").values();
  auto size=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(),power=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  size.lines(n,plain.numeric_column("exact_size").values(),"exact",{37,99,235}).lines(n,plain.numeric_column("score_size").values(),"score",{220,38,38}).lines(n,corrected.numeric_column("score_size").values(),"corrected score",{22,163,74});
  size.abline_h(.05).title("Actual null rejection probability").x_label("trials n").y_label("size at p = 0.1").y_limits(0,.15);
  power.lines(n,plain.numeric_column("exact_power").values(),"exact",{37,99,235}).lines(n,plain.numeric_column("score_power").values(),"score",{220,38,38}).lines(n,corrected.numeric_column("score_power").values(),"corrected score",{22,163,74});
  power.title("Power at true p = 0.3").x_label("trials n").y_label("rejection probability").y_limits(0,1.4);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(size).add(power).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/hypothesis_calibration.svg");
  std::print("Uncorrected score comparison:\n\n{}\n\nCorrected score comparison:\n\n{}\n",dm::stats::binomial_operating_characteristics({1,5,10,20,40,80},.1,.3,.05,dm::stats::Alternative::TwoSided,false).to_md(6),dm::stats::binomial_operating_characteristics({1,5,10,20,40,80},.1,.3).to_md(6));
}
```
