\caption(Size and power are sums over every possible binomial count for each sample size. The horizontal line is the nominal 5% size, not a target for power.)
\alttext(Three jagged curves compare exact, uncorrected score and corrected score rules. The size panel reveals sparse-count approximation errors; the power panel evaluates the fixed rules at true probability 0.3.)

At five trials, exact size is 0.00856 and uncorrected score size is 0.08146; their
powers at p = 0.3 are 0.16308 and 0.47178. That higher apparent power accompanies
excess null rejection. At twenty trials the exact rule has size 0.04317 and power
0.76249; the corrected-score rule is more conservative here, with size 0.01125 and
power 0.58363.

> Iris measurements

>> Dataset, location effects and a declared family

`dm::datasets::iris()` contains 150 flowers, 50 of each species, with four complete
measurements in centimetres and a species label. We first summarize the data, then
compare setosa and versicolor petal length with Welch and rank-sum tests, and all three
species with classical ANOVA and Kruskal–Wallis. The F-test compares setosa/virginica
petal-length variance. These are distinct questions, not five replications of one test.
ANOVA's common-variance assumption is questionable for these petal lengths; its result
is presented as a model calculation rather than made robust by another test agreeing.

Separately, we declare the four Welch comparisons of versicolor minus virginica on all
four measurements as one multiplicity family and apply Holm. The plot and table give
raw-unit mean differences and marginal 95% intervals. The historically collected,
species-stratified data are not randomized treatments; these calculations are a sampling-
model illustration, not evidence for a causal species effect or a new confirmatory study.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hypothesis_iris_location.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
const auto d=dm::datasets::iris();const auto setosa=d.filter([](const auto& r){return r.get_string("Species")=="setosa";}),versicolor=d.filter([](const auto& r){return r.get_string("Species")=="versicolor";}),virginica=d.filter([](const auto& r){return r.get_string("Species")=="virginica";});
  const auto a=setosa.numeric_column("Petal.Length").values(),b=versicolor.numeric_column("Petal.Length").values(),c=virginica.numeric_column("Petal.Length").values();
  std::vector<double> pooled=a;pooled.insert(pooled.end(),b.begin(),b.end());pooled.insert(pooled.end(),c.begin(),c.end());
  const auto summary=d|dm::group_by("Species")|dm::summarise(dm::agg::n("n"),dm::agg::mean("Petal.Length","petal_mean_cm"),dm::agg::sd("Petal.Length","petal_sd_cm"));
  const auto location=dm::stats::hypothesis_test_table({dm::stats::t_test_two_sample(a,b),dm::stats::wilcoxon_rank_sum_test(a,b),dm::stats::one_way_anova(pooled,{50,50,50}),dm::stats::kruskal_wallis_test(pooled,{50,50,50}),dm::stats::f_test_variance(a,c)},{"Welch setosa-versicolor","rank-sum setosa-versicolor","ANOVA three species","Kruskal-Wallis three species","variance setosa/virginica"});
  const std::vector<std::string> features={"Sepal.Length","Sepal.Width","Petal.Length","Petal.Width"};std::vector<dm::stats::HypothesisTestResult> tests;std::vector<double> raw,effect,lo,hi;
  for(const auto& f:features){auto t=dm::stats::t_test_two_sample(versicolor.numeric_column(f).values(),virginica.numeric_column(f).values());tests.push_back(t);raw.push_back(t.p_value);effect.push_back(t.estimate1-t.estimate2);lo.push_back(t.conf_int_lower);hi.push_back(t.conf_int_upper);}
  auto family=dm::stats::hypothesis_test_table(tests,features);family.add_column("mean_difference_cm",effect);family.add_column("holm_p",dm::stats::p_adjust(raw,dm::stats::PAdjustMethod::Holm));
  auto boxes=dm::graphics::RPlot::boxplot({a,b,c},{"setosa","versicolor","virginica"});boxes.title("Petal lengths by species").x_label("species").y_label("petal length (cm)").y_limits(0,9);
  auto intervals=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();intervals.segments(lo,{4,3,2,1},hi,{4,3,2,1},{37,99,235},2).points(effect,{4,3,2,1},"mean; 95% CI",{37,99,235});
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<features.size();++i)intervals.text(-2.05,4.3-i,features[i]);
  intervals.abline_v(0).title("Versicolor minus virginica").x_label("mean difference (cm)").y_label("feature row").x_limits(-2.5,.65).y_limits(.3,5.5);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(boxes).add(intervals).size(1100,510).fit_panels().save_svg("build/hypothesis_iris_location.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions fmt;fmt.digits=6;fmt.scientific={"p_value","holm_p"};
  std::print("{} flowers, {} columns.\n\n{}\n\n{}\n\nFour-comparison family:\n\n{}\n",d.nrows(),d.ncols(),summary|dm::to_md(4),location.select({"label","statistic","p_value"}).to_md(fmt),family.select({"label","mean_difference_cm","ci_lower","ci_upper","p_value","holm_p"}).to_md(fmt));
}
```
