\caption(The pooled association combines species separation with association among flowers of the same species. Correlation coefficients and sampling-model p-values are distinct outputs.)
\alttext(A sepal-versus-petal scatter is coloured by species. Tables compare pooled Pearson and Spearman coefficients with each species-specific coefficient and label the Spearman approximation.)

Pooled Pearson r is 0.872, versus 0.267 within setosa, 0.754 within versicolor and
0.864 within virginica. For setosa the Pearson and approximate Spearman p-values lie
on opposite sides of 0.05 (0.06070 and 0.04985); that small threshold difference does
not justify choosing the method after seeing its result. The coefficients and intervals
carry more information than a binary significance label.

>> Shape diagnostics with a stated calibration limitation

Setosa sepal lengths are recorded to one decimal place. Their normal QQ plot and
Shapiro–Francia statistic describe shape, with the latter's continuous-normal p-value
only an approximation for rounded data. Failure to reject is not proof that normality
holds, and it is not a licence to ignore the design or variance assumptions of other tests.

For KS we use the illustrative fixed benchmark \(N(5,0.4^2)\), in centimetres.
We display its D statistic only: rounding already violates a continuous-null model, and
this historically selected dataset is not a prospective test of that benchmark. In
particular, replacing the benchmark by the sample's fitted mean and SD would not turn
the ordinary KS p-value into a valid composite-normality test.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hypothesis_iris_shape.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
const auto d=dm::datasets::iris().filter([](const auto& r){return r.get_string("Species")=="setosa";});const auto x=d.numeric_column("Sepal.Length").values();
  const auto sf=dm::stats::shapiro_francia_test(x),ks=dm::stats::ks_test_one_sample_normal(x,5,.4);
  auto qq=dm::graphics::RPlot::qqnorm(x,"setosa sepal length");qq.qqline(x).title("Rounded setosa measurements: normal QQ").x_label("standard-normal quantile").y_label("sepal length (cm)").y_limits(4.1,6.6);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");qq.size(820,470).save_svg("build/hypothesis_iris_shape.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame benchmark;benchmark.add_column("reference_mean_cm",std::vector<double>{5});benchmark.add_column("reference_sd_cm",std::vector<double>{.4});benchmark.add_column("D_only",std::vector<double>{ks.statistic});
  std::print("Continuous-normal approximation on rounded measurements:\n\n{}\n\nFixed-benchmark discrepancy (no calibrated p-value claimed):\n\n{}\n",dm::stats::hypothesis_test_table({sf}).select({"method","statistic","p_value"}).to_md(6),benchmark.to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Continuous-normal approximation on rounded measurements:

| method | statistic | p_value |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| Shapiro-Francia normality test | 0.981695 | 0.535688 |


Fixed-benchmark discrepancy (no calibrated p-value claimed):

| reference_mean_cm | reference_sd_cm | D_only |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 5 | 0.4 | 0.121294 |

\image(figures/hypothesis_iris_shape.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Normal QQ diagnostic for rounded measurements. The KS discrepancy is shown without interpreting a nominal continuous-null p-value.)
\alttext(Ordered setosa sepal lengths lie near a normal reference line with visible ties. Tables report the Shapiro-Francia approximation and the distance to a separately specified normal benchmark.)

>> Counts after an explicit measurement threshold

We define long petals as strictly greater than 4.5 cm and compare versicolor with
virginica. This fixed illustrative cutoff is not the pooled sample median and is not
optimized over candidate thresholds. Dichotomization discards measurement information;
it does not create a second independent experiment. We show the 2x2 counts, Pearson
and Fisher results, then ask the separate one-sample question whether the versicolor
long-petal proportion equals one half using score and exact binomial tests. The
reference sampling model treats flowers as independent within their sampled species;
the balanced species counts themselves are not a random prevalence estimate.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hypothesis_iris_counts.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
const auto d=dm::datasets::iris();std::vector<std::size_t> longer,shorter;
  for(const auto& species:{std::string("versicolor"),std::string("virginica")}) {const auto g=d.filter([&](const auto& r){return r.get_string("Species")==species;});const auto long_rows=g.filter([](const auto& r){return r.get_double("Petal.Length")>4.5;});longer.push_back(long_rows.nrows());shorter.push_back(g.nrows()-long_rows.nrows());}
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame counts;counts.add_column("species",std::vector<std::string>{"versicolor","virginica"});counts.add_column("long",std::vector<double>{double(longer[0]),double(longer[1])});counts.add_column("not_long",std::vector<double>{double(shorter[0]),double(shorter[1])});
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();plot.bars({.8,1.8},{double(longer[0]),double(longer[1])},"petal > 4.5 cm",{37,99,235},.35).bars({1.2,2.2},{double(shorter[0]),double(shorter[1])},"petal <= 4.5 cm",{220,38,38},.35);
  plot.title("An explicit petal-length cutoff").x_label("1: versicolor; 2: virginica").y_label("flower count").x_limits(.4,2.6).y_limits(0,70);
  const auto tests=dm::stats::hypothesis_test_table({dm::stats::chi_squared_test_independence({double(longer[0]),double(shorter[0]),double(longer[1]),double(shorter[1])},2,2),dm::stats::fisher_exact_test_2x2(longer[0],shorter[0],longer[1],shorter[1]),dm::stats::proportion_test_one_sample(longer[0],50),dm::stats::binomial_test(longer[0],50)},{"Yates association","Fisher association","versicolor score vs 0.5","versicolor exact vs 0.5"});
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");plot.size(820,470).save_svg("build/hypothesis_iris_counts.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions fmt;fmt.digits=6;fmt.scientific={"p_value"};std::print("{}\n\nAssociation:\n\n{}\n\nSample odds ratio: {:.6f}\n\nVersicolor proportion:\n\n{}\n",counts.to_md(),tests.head(2).select({"label","statistic","p_value"}).to_md(fmt),tests.double_at("estimate1",1),tests.tail(2).select({"label","estimate1","ci_lower","ci_upper","p_value"}).to_md(fmt));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| species | long | not_long |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| versicolor | 14 | 36 |
| virginica | 49 | 1 |


Association:

| label | statistic | p_value |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| Yates association | 49.592450 | 1.892406e-12 |
| Fisher association | 14.000000 | 2.762967e-14 |


Sample odds ratio: 0.007937

Versicolor proportion:

| label | estimate1 | ci_lower | ci_upper | p_value |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| versicolor score vs 0.5 | 0.28 | 0.174742 | 0.416651 | 2.979467e-03 |
| versicolor exact vs 0.5 | 0.28 | 0.162311 | 0.424905 | 2.602171e-03 |

\image(figures/hypothesis_iris_counts.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Observed long/not-long counts under a stated 4.5 cm rule. The conditional association and one-sample proportion tests answer different questions on overlapping data.)
\alttext(Two pairs of coloured bars show long and not-long counts for versicolor and virginica. Tables give exact counts, association tests and the versicolor proportion tests with their distinct interval definitions.)

There are 14 long petals among 50 versicolor flowers and 49 among 50 virginica
flowers. Their sample odds ratio is 0.00794, with Fisher conditional p-value about
2.76e-14. The separate versicolor proportion is 0.28, with exact 95% interval
[0.16231, 0.42491] and a two-sided binomial p-value of 0.00260 against one half.
The one-sample score and exact intervals differ as their definitions predict.

> Conclusion

A test result is interpretable only with its null, sampling design, ordering and reference
calibration. The worked examples separate mean, rank and conditional-count questions;
finite enumeration reveals conservative exact rules and sparse-count approximation errors.
Confidence intervals add effect units and uncertainty but must be matched to their actual
construction. Iris illustrates species-stratified effects, a declared multiplicity family,
and the difference between pooled association and within-species relationships. Agreement
among several p-values neither validates their assumptions nor supplies causal evidence.

> References

- Student (1908). [The Probable Error of a Mean|https://doi.org/10.1093/biomet/6.1.1]. ~Biometrika~, 6(1), 1–25. Student reference; see also the documented Welch variant in [R t.test|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/t.test.html].
- R Core Team. [wilcox.test|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/wilcox.test.html], [oneway.test|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/oneway.test.html] and [kruskal.test|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/kruskal.test.html]. Nulls, rank conventions and reference methods. Here Wilcoxon is always a normal approximation and one_way_anova is the equal-variance procedure.
- Clopper, C. J. and Pearson, E. S. (1934). [The Use of Confidence or Fiducial Limits Illustrated in the Case of the Binomial|https://doi.org/10.1093/biomet/26.4.404]. ~Biometrika~, 26(4), 404–413; [R binom.test|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/binom.test.html].
- R Core Team. [fisher.test|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/fisher.test.html], [chisq.test|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/chisq.test.html] and [prop.test|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/prop.test.html]. Probability ordering, conditional sampling and score-test conventions; the library's one-proportion Wilson interval is uncorrected.
- R Core Team. [cor.test|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/cor.test.html], [var.test|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/var.test.html] and [ks.test|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/ks.test.html]. Correlation/variance references and the fixed-parameter KS requirement; see also [nortest sf.test|https://search.r-project.org/CRAN/refmans/nortest/html/sf.test.html] for the Shapiro–Francia/Royston approximation.
- Holm, S. (1979). [A Simple Sequentially Rejective Multiple Test Procedure|https://www.jstor.org/stable/4615733]. ~Scandinavian Journal of Statistics~, 6(2), 65–70; [R p.adjust|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/p.adjust.html].
- Fisher, R. A. (1936). [The use of multiple measurements in taxonomic problems|https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-1809.1936.tb02137.x]. ~Annals of Eugenics~, 7(2), 179–188. Iris measurements used directly from the built-in dataset.

